examples.solution_text:
Transfer both given angles to the lower parallel line using alternate angles.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);\coordinate (L) at ({-2/tan(43)},2);\coordinate (R) at ({2/tan(72)},2);
\draw (-2.8,2)--(2,2);\draw (-2.8,0)--(2,0);\draw (L)--(P)--(R);
\draw[->] (-2.7,2)--(-2.2,2);\draw[->] (-2.7,0)--(-2.2,0);
\draw (L)++(-43:0.5) arc (-43:0:0.5);\path (L)++(-21.5:0.92) node {$43^{\circ}$};
\draw (R)++(180:0.45) arc (180:252:0.45);\path (R)++(216:0.8) node {$72^{\circ}$};
\draw (P)++(137:0.55) arc (137:180:0.55);\path (P)++(158.5:1) node {$43^{\circ}$};
\draw (P)++(0:0.55) arc (0:72:0.55);\path (P)++(36:0.95) node {$72^{\circ}$};
\draw (P)++(72:0.55) arc (72:137:0.55);\path (P)++(104.5:0.85) node {$x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Angles on a straight line add to $180^{\circ}$.
$\begin{aligned}43^{\circ}+x+72^{\circ}&=180^{\circ}\\x&=180^{\circ}-43^{\circ}-72^{\circ}\\x&=65^{\circ}\end{aligned}$

