examples.solution_text:
Construct a horizontal line through $P$, parallel to the given lines.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);\coordinate (A) at ({1.4/tan(32)},1.4);\coordinate (B) at ({1.4/tan(46)},-1.4);
\draw (-0.8,1.4)--(3,1.4);\draw (-0.8,-1.4)--(3,-1.4);\draw (A)--(P)--(B);
\draw[dashed] (-0.8,0)--(3,0);
\draw[->] (-0.6,1.4)--(0,1.4);\draw[->] (-0.6,-1.4)--(0,-1.4);\draw[->] (-0.6,0)--(0,0);
\path (P) node[below left] {$P$};
\draw (A)++(180:0.5) arc (180:212:0.5);\path (A)++(196:1) node {$32^{\circ}$};
\draw (B)++(134:0.5) arc (134:180:0.5);\path (B)++(157:0.95) node {$46^{\circ}$};
\draw (P)++(0:0.6) arc (0:32:0.6);\path (P)++(16:1.25) node {$32^{\circ}$};
\draw (P)++(-46:0.6) arc (-46:0:0.6);\path (P)++(-23:1.05) node {$46^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Alternate angles on parallel lines give the two adjacent parts of $x$ as $32^{\circ}$ and $46^{\circ}$.
$\begin{aligned}x&=32^{\circ}+46^{\circ}\\x&=78^{\circ}\end{aligned}$
